            {
              "id": "glog-57b499fd-f5c8-4de5-9fcb-a68462ed82cb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory beyond the intended boundary, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This can result in crashes, incorrect behavior, or potential execution of malicious code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Be careful when manipulating strings, especially when using functions like `strcpy()`, `strcat()`, and `strlen()`, which do not check for buffer overflows.\n\nWhen possible, use safer alternatives that include a length parameter, such as `strnlen()`, `strncpy()`, and `strncat()`, which can prevent buffer overflows by not reading or writing past a specified length.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strlen(kind)`, use `strnlen(kind, MAX_LENGTH)`, where `MAX_LENGTH` is the maximum length of the string you expect. This will prevent `strnlen()` from reading beyond the end of the string and causing a buffer overflow.\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n\n#define MAX_LENGTH 100\n\n// ...\n\nsize_t length = strnlen(kind, MAX_LENGTH);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` and `strnlen()` functions are part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `<string.h>` header file.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
