                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string up to, but not including, the null-terminating character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null byte, potentially leading to buffer overflows or other memory corruption issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen`, such as `strnlen`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read past this length, even if it does not encounter a null byte.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strlen(\"-check-prefix=\")`, consider using `sizeof(\"-check-prefix=\") - 1`. The `sizeof` operator returns the size of the string including the null-terminating character, so subtracting 1 gives the length of the string.\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = sizeof(\"-check-prefix=\") - 1;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed to use it.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
